The workbench page itself: what it links, what it declares, how it installs.

- Overlay assets: narrow.css / touch.css / fonts.css, and shell.js.
- The shared viewport contract (viewport-fit=cover,
  interactive-widget=resizes-content) and a first-paint brand background, so the
  page never flashes white before the theme applies.
- The favicon set: sized ICO fallback first, adaptive SVG last, with favicon.js
  swapping the scheme-pinned pair (Chromium rasterizes a favicon once per URL
  and never re-runs its embedded media query). favicon.js is the one the auth
  and error pages already load, from the same /_static path, not a second copy.
- The installed app: standalone display, not fullscreen, so the clock and
  battery stay visible; window-controls-overlay drops with it because our
  titlebar reserves no titlebar-area-* room. Brand name (the
  apple-mobile-web-app-title iOS actually shows), description and colours are
  pinned to packages/shared by the "PWA install metadata" test. The manifest
  carries one colour for the Android splash (the dark tile of the launcher
  icon); theme-color metas then track the scheme per prefers-color-scheme.

Two neighbouring pages are deliberately left alone: workbench-dev.html, served
only when the environment is not a built one and so never by our release image;
and callback.html, which has no input to keep clear of a keyboard and no
safe-area padding, so viewport-fit=cover there would only move its text under
the notch upstream's default keeps it out of.

